---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About | HerbGuide"
  description="About HerbGuide and the editorial approach behind its practical Traditional Chinese herb content."
  structuredData={[
    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "AboutPage",
      name: "About HerbGuide",
      description:
        "About HerbGuide and the editorial approach behind its practical Traditional Chinese herb content.",
      url: "https://herbguide.store/about/",
    },
  ]}
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="shell narrow prose-block">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>A practical guide to Chinese herbs and kitchen traditions</h1>
      <p>
        HerbGuide is being built as a lightweight editorial site for readers who want a
        clearer introduction to Chinese herbs, TCM concepts, and kitchen traditions.
      </p>
      <p>
        The guiding style is simple: keep the writing warm, practical, and careful. Explain
        specialized Chinese terms in plain English. Highlight food uses where appropriate.
        Keep safety boundaries visible.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Editorial mission</p>
        <h2>What this site is trying to do well</h2>
        <p>
          Many English-language herb sites swing too far in one direction. Some are so
          academic that ordinary readers bounce off immediately. Others are so promotional
          that every herb sounds like a miracle. HerbGuide is built for the middle path:
          clear, useful, culturally grounded, and calm.
        </p>
        <p>
          We want readers to understand what a herb is, how TCM describes it, how it shows
          up in kitchen traditions, what the important cautions are, and where traditional ideas
          begin and modern evidence still remains limited.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Who it is for</p>
        <h2>Curious readers, not only practitioners</h2>
        <p>
          This site is meant for people who are interested in Chinese herbs but do not want
          to feel lost in untranslated jargon. That includes home cooks, curious readers,
          diaspora readers exploring family traditions, and beginners who simply want a more
          trustworthy introduction.
        </p>
        <p>
          American English is the primary voice of the site, but we keep Chinese names,
          pinyin, and core terms visible so readers can connect the English explanation back
          to the original tradition.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block list-card">
        <h2>Why Chinese food traditions matter on HerbGuide</h2>
        <p>
          Food traditions are often the most approachable doorway into Chinese herb culture.
          Many ingredients sit naturally between kitchen and materia medica: ginger, goji,
          jujube, lotus seed, black sesame, pear, mint, and chrysanthemum are all easier to
          explain than a shelf full of concentrated products.
        </p>
        <p>
          That practical entry point also fits the lightweight, trustworthy launch the site
          is aiming for on Cloudflare Pages.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block list-card">
        <h2>How this Chinese herbs website will grow</h2>
        <p>
          The first stage is a clean educational library with solid herb pages, core TCM
          explainers, introductory classics content, and food tradition articles. Later
          growth can include a newsletter, richer research summaries, internal topic hubs,
          and more advanced herb comparisons.
        </p>
        <p>
          The key is to grow without losing editorial discipline. More pages should mean
          more clarity, not more noise.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Brand clarity</p>
        <h2>About this Chinese herbs and kitchen traditions website</h2>
        <p>
          HerbGuide is being built as a long-term educational resource about Chinese herbs,
          traditional food knowledge, basic TCM concepts, and the classical texts behind this
          tradition. The tone is intentionally friendly and practical rather than clinical or
          mystical.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Reader fit</p>
        <h2>Who should read HerbGuide?</h2>
        <p>
          This site is especially useful for readers searching for beginner-friendly Chinese
          herbs information, plain-English explanations of TCM vocabulary, and practical
          kitchen ideas that feel culturally grounded without making medical promises or
          personal recommendations.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Reader questions</p>
        <h2>About HerbGuide FAQ</h2>
        <h3>Is HerbGuide a medical advice website?</h3>
        <p>
          No. HerbGuide is an educational site about Chinese herbs, food culture, and TCM
          language written for curious readers in plain American English.
        </p>
        <h3>Why is the site focused on herbs and kitchen traditions first?</h3>
        <p>
          Because these are the most approachable entry points for many readers. They allow
          the site to stay practical, visually grounded, and easier to trust.
        </p>
        <h3>Will HerbGuide add premium or subscriber content later?</h3>
        <p>
          It may, especially for deeper recipe structures and more advanced
          educational guides, but the current public site is focused on strong free content
          first.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Keep exploring</p>
        <h2>Where new readers should start</h2>
        <p>
          Start with the <a href="/herbs/">Herb Library</a> if you want ingredient profiles,
          <a href="/food-therapy/">Food Therapy</a> if you want kitchen ideas, and
          <a href="/basics/">TCM Basics</a> if you want terminology explained.
        </p>
        <p>
          Readers who want curated paths instead of section lists should continue to
          <a href="/topics/"> Topics</a> and <a href="/glossary/">TCM Glossary</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Readers who prefer side-by-side ingredient learning should use
          <a href="/basics/"> TCM Basics</a> and start with the comparison guides there.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
